DistroForge Debian packaging
=============================

This package uses dh-sequence-python3 with pybuild-plugin-pyproject.

The Git baseline was imported from the verified 0.3.5-1 package. The develop
branch restores the 0.3.5-2 runtime and documentation delta from the preserved
binary release artifact described in PROVENANCE.md, while retaining later
repository CI fixes. The Ubuntu resolute Standards-Version adjustment is
carried directly in the source, with no active Quilt patches.

Deliberate deviations from lintian
---------------------------------

The package carries no lintian overrides. One pedantic tag is left standing on
purpose rather than silenced:

  redundant-rules-requires-root-no-field
    Rules-Requires-Root: no has been the dpkg default since 1.22.13, so the field
    is redundant on a current toolchain. It stays because "this package never
    needs root to build" is a contract here, not a side effect of which dpkg ran
    the build: two tests assert the field and four modules cite it to justify
    staying rootless. Deleting it to quiet a pedantic tag would make the contract
    depend on the builder's dpkg version.

Manual pages, the desktop entry and the launcher icon live in share/ in the
upstream tree, not in debian/, because upstream and the packaging are the same
project -- which is what maintainer-manual-page and maintainer-desktop-entry ask
for.

debian/watch carries comments and no rule. Upstream publishes no tarball, and
upstream is this same repository, so there is nothing for uscan to poll that is
not this package itself; a rule resolving to nothing would be worse than a file
stating the situation. Packaging versions are tagged debian/<version> from
0.3.5-16 onward and that changes none of it, for the reasons the watch file
gives. Note that pgpmode=gittag, the obvious way to verify a signed release tag,
is not implemented by the devscripts in resolute (uscan 2.26.7).

Release tags
------------

debian/gbp.conf pins the tag naming (DEP-14's debian/<version>) and pins
sign-tags, because gbp passes --no-sign when that option is unset and would
otherwise override tag.gpgsign in the repository config. `make tag` cuts one
through tools/release-tag.sh; docs/packaging-release.md says what it refuses and
why publishing the tag is a separate command. Versions before 0.3.5-16 are not
retro-tagged: this repository's history starts at an imported 0.3.5-1 baseline,
so most of the earlier changelog versions have no commit here to point at.

Standalone development remains driven by pyproject.toml and a local virtual
environment. Debian package builds should use distribution Python packages from
debian/control instead of pip-installed dependencies.

During alpha development, local binary package builds are intentionally outside
the default workflow. Use lint, tests, import checks and static packaging review
unless the maintainer explicitly asks for a package build in the current task.

For a source upload, create an upstream orig tarball that excludes local build
artifacts such as .venv, __pycache__, .pytest_cache, .ruff_cache and egg-info.

If build dependency inspection is needed, review the dependencies listed in
debian/control without producing package artifacts.

The maintainer address
----------------------

debian/control names a forwarding alias, github@distroforge.anonaddy.com, and not
a personal mailbox: the field is republished in every archive index and package
page, so it is a public address by construction, and an alias can be rotated
without touching the mailbox behind it. Every entry up to 0.3.5-9 named
maintainers@distroforge.invalid instead, a domain RFC 2606 reserves so that it
can never resolve.

Policy 3.3 asks for more than a well-formed field. The address "must accept mail
from those role accounts in Debian used to send automated mails regarding the
package", which the section spells out as non-spam mail from the bug-tracking
system, all mail from the archive maintenance software, and other role accounts
commonly agreed on by the project. Rejecting that mail is a serious bug and not
an untidiness: #1063752 was filed at that severity because a maintainer address
bounced ftpmaster over DMARC.

Two tests in tests/test_policy_compliance.py hold the halves of this a machine
can check with no network -- that the address is in no RFC 2606 reserved TLD and
is no GitHub noreply, and that _DEB_MAINTAINER in core/desktop_source.py still
agrees with debian/control. Neither proves delivery. Before an upload, send one
message to the address from a mailbox outside the forwarding provider, confirm it
arrives, and confirm that nothing at the destination files mail from an unknown
role account as spam. That check belongs to the upload and not to the build: it
needs the network and a real mailbox, and it goes stale whenever the alias is
rotated or the filtering behind it changes.

Since 2026-07-27 the signing key carries a user id for this same address, which is
what lets debsign select the key from a changelog trailer and lets a published
keyring name the maintainer it authenticates for. How that keyring is exported
without carrying the personal user id alongside it is in docs/packaging-release.md.
